  "problem": "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^{10}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places.\n\n$(1.15)^{10}\u2248$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+\u22ef\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}\u22481+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{10} & =(1+0.15)^{10} \\\\  & \u22481+10(0.15)+45(0.15)^{2}+120(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+10(0.15)+45(0.022\\,5)+120(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+1.5+1.012\\,5+0.405 \\\\  & \u22483.917\\,5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.",
